#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156994 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157393 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c799d6-e3fe-5b78-986d-f2068b2db058" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "65957407-b302-51c5-93d9-0f71f3517362" :question/math-academy-id "e-10527" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2}{3},$ compute $\\textrm{E}[5X^2 - 2X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\textrm{E}[X] + b\\textrm{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5{X}^{2}-2X] & =5\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\textrm E [X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5{X}^{2}-2X] & =5\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156995 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bada6a7f-c02e-51cd-8ea2-0594a94648a1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "292610ec-9c27-590d-b207-c76adc959d55" :question/math-academy-id "e-10528" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n![](images/f3/f3149524cf7975083139c82369095a564c5cb28e66ecd1bdab94721c64bd6d21.svg)\n\nCalculate $\\textrm{E}[X^2].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of $X^2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_{S} x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}\\,{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0}+\\frac{3}{8}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(0+8)+\\frac{3}{8}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156996 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Higher Moments of a Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "4c5666a4-f477-5b1a-8d17-1e2aa594863f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10529" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n![](images/19/1940f446dbc6c352b86f413501008abd7ac519a79cfa6be9b7207ecfe13609a7.svg)\n\nCalculate $\\textrm E [X^4].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_{S} x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\cdot (1-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5}\\,{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{6}\\,{x}^{6})\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{10}\\,{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{6}-0)+\\frac{1}{10}(32-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{31}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{47}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157393 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c799d6-e3fe-5b78-986d-f2068b2db058" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b58a9e1-f2e5-5bab-86c3-1dc62a5bf1dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434407} {:answer/id #uuid "83994b5b-dec3-525b-9473-71abebd50a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434408} {:answer/id #uuid "908f1924-a4cc-5cae-83ec-6a4c7872904f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434410} {:answer/id #uuid "93d5d0a4-79ad-5a4d-854e-315f284a4eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434411} {:answer/id #uuid "a25f9bac-857f-5e6a-8fee-76b7ccf2820e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83994b5b-dec3-525b-9473-71abebd50a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434408} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c9c34c2-a686-5dff-8116-1e613117e2fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf5c8a43-29b3-589c-9d23-ef6cc2a81919" :question/math-academy-id "q-131142" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,1<x<3\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ compute $\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}]=5\\text{E}[X]-3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\,|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=2$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{13}{3}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[5X-3{X}^{2}] & =5\\text{E}[X]-3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2-3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\  & =10-13 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bada6a7f-c02e-51cd-8ea2-0594a94648a1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b6e4f24-584e-5287-9823-f5033de3e1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434404} {:answer/id #uuid "779d58b8-71da-535f-bbb4-074445fcafb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434405} {:answer/id #uuid "abaaaacf-5ed0-57a2-9267-81811b57a5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434413} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f4ebd2-8d57-5504-991d-9387171672e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434418} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1929e9-31d9-5271-b335-170151a22db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6f4ebd2-8d57-5504-991d-9387171672e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434418} :answer-field/id #uuid "24eb573e-08ea-570d-9301-8fa6561f1ac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1867bd8a-68ea-5fbb-a8c0-a7b43c7381ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-152897" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x, & 0<x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\,x, & 1\\le x<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{12}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0)+\\frac{1}{12}(16-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bada6a7f-c02e-51cd-8ea2-0594a94648a1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Second Moment of a Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1787c976-b394-5db2-9647-7788ad9018da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427281} {:answer/id #uuid "17b6397b-6d8a-502f-b1bb-d6805bac1e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427282} {:answer/id #uuid "69257811-1577-5b8b-814f-a4ffc4c0084f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427304} {:answer/id #uuid "8386f4fd-561f-5977-a7b8-f5fa721f4d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427313} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5e19b7-9577-599a-b29a-d5fbd4562df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec5e19b7-9577-599a-b29a-d5fbd4562df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427341} :answer-field/id #uuid "03eb10c9-9e29-5bb8-8db9-ad1064b9deaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84598522-637e-5d77-b555-f1f4d414ed22" :question/math-academy-id "q-152899" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & -1<x<0, \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\,x, & 0\\le x<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{4}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+1)+\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Higher Moments of a Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208a0b10-d20c-5680-b78b-191ae08c910b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427287} {:answer/id #uuid "92ac3001-cb5a-5ac0-aae9-33678855783b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427318} {:answer/id #uuid "99ed83c0-56e7-5eae-a55b-c24cc9289617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427319} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3014be-44fd-57b1-b8a4-3fe31cb19de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427337} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2e1283-3791-58ca-a36b-00de002d3091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99ed83c0-56e7-5eae-a55b-c24cc9289617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427319} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7aca109-b18a-57b5-90ec-bf719ba83078" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2dabf35b-e60b-5d7b-a653-f44f57203be2" :question/math-academy-id "q-152916" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,1\\le x\\le 2.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{5}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fifth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{5}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(16-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157395 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88bd32d5-4a84-58b3-a3e9-73bf32cb5e4f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Higher Moments of a Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8146a1-89ab-5805-b0d1-3cb41fad7d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{8}-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434401} {:answer/id #uuid "77b5e97e-bc6c-5d70-b0ab-61c62f43eefe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434406} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c4a89b-c4d2-5db7-98e5-df1b9a18f200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434414} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd85839-7391-5b9a-80bf-f734af24df0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434415} {:answer/id #uuid "c0834f80-0b5a-5af1-a67a-b29a8e35f4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d8146a1-89ab-5805-b0d1-3cb41fad7d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{8}-1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434401} :answer-field/id #uuid "29094946-ea57-508c-a265-54b76fd27fdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a18a122-3ad0-51db-8855-5d861bdec3e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-152917" :question/problem "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,1\\le x\\le {e}^{2}.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({e}^{8}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{8}-1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157393 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c799d6-e3fe-5b78-986d-f2068b2db058" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Transformed Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d29d5bc-decf-59aa-b33c-d8a3d6abb2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434402} {:answer/id #uuid "6acb02d9-6f5a-5313-a4d3-7591b95228cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434403} {:answer/id #uuid "8e261157-b7ed-5a7f-a989-f68de2600a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434409} {:answer/id #uuid "c189e133-767c-5164-a4a2-027bd0af6f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434417} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fd88a3-2459-5bec-829b-d2f05e7cacbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d29d5bc-decf-59aa-b33c-d8a3d6abb2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434402} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b5a9c84-ed66-57e2-9943-2424b914876f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc2748a6-b91a-5b23-b40c-5cede2d8d71d" :question/math-academy-id "q-90394" :question/problem "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[3{X}^{2}+4].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=2-2x,\\,0<x<1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[3{X}^{2}+4]=3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+4\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot (2-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{1}{6}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3{X}^{2}+4] & =3\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+4 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
